#{[17592186394289 #edb/ref 1080 "images/cf/cf449d44237c85963bf4fbb134da290b39aac4ebd0b128313052cbd1970db67f.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394289 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394289 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d69be06f-7ba5-5d06-aad7-b3459d303317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394290 #edb/ref 1080 "images/5b/5b4684361e541532d1be24de046f17201811146fbb0413222c5100e5a3eb80dc.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ef8d4dc-0422-509e-9d94-1b349ccfac6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394627 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394627 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85044" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394627 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394627 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate $(\\begin{aligned}20 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394627 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ccb665c6-b272-5591-95cb-e38be54fe76e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394627 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that $(\\frac{20}{16})$ means the same thing as ${}_{20}{C}_{16}.$\n\n In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=20$ and $r=16,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{20}{C}_{16} & =\\frac{20!}{(20-16)!16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20!}{4!16!} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17\\cdot \\cancel{16!}}{4!\\cancel{16!}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\cdot 19\\cdot 18\\cdot 17}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{116\\,280}{24} \\\\  & =4\\,845.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394628 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ee128c64-8462-52f2-aa05-6a02c9deb300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394629 #edb/ref 1080 "19\\,380" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394629 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394629 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "43fa39e5-ce50-500d-851f-de9eb3b12eda" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394630 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\,480" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394630 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394630 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b8e55eef-a2a6-5dcc-8dbd-442ffb6f3b06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394631 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\,860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394631 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394631 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "24b6f013-8ec7-5811-963d-26ce3311756c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394632 #edb/ref 1080 "9\\,280" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394632 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394632 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cde0fe8a-36a8-5e30-8de3-aa6250e1a52c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394633 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\,845" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394633 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394633 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9dca18c-0cda-53f2-ae56-98a74295c88b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394827 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394827 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90174" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394827 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394827 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the diagrams shows the solution of the following system?\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}y\\le 2x \\\\ y\\le -2x\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "12b718de-1f28-5ec6-b73e-0c704e48e717" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394827 #edb/ref 1074 "To graph the first inequality $y\\le 2x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=2x.$\n\n- Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *below* the line.\n\nTo graph the second inequality $y\\le -2x,$ we draw the corresponding straight line $y=-2x.$ \n\n- Since we have an \"or equal to\" inequality, the line itself is included in the solution, so the line is solid.\n\n- The inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* or equal to those on the line, so we shade *below* the line.\n\nThe intersection of the solutions is the following:\n\n![](images/43/438928da5e9de5e9ba3d2abbe06b6f1f8b6b0598a0b82bec1f51824b9ef72a71.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394828 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "7c907422-6a43-51a3-a689-8ba380b4f66e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394829 #edb/ref 1080 "images/bb/bb0ea125af03527a6e3111fc638f5fd9a4efaa39bc7b48a82e2efcfcfb0b696b.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394829 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394829 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "338a182a-eee2-5984-9c16-ac8f5df92c94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394830 #edb/ref 1080 "images/27/27ba3ad2783e64a6b29d5ea488d63fc634d2a43ee01d6a77c4802da8bc06b641.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394830 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394830 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3faab0ae-2527-51e9-960b-f170f93df96e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394831 #edb/ref 1080 "images/e5/e5428cead5e96d930e52a833942a1c4452c43f3e2f6ba7c6fc39758ee83e8d81.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394831 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394831 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6ca9e1d-7076-5b73-a120-7f365e92f4c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394832 #edb/ref 1080 "images/19/197ae7933ece17a22bdf17c2397053541891a789892813599930de273496cd78.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394832 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394832 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "55b6c46d-d1d1-5590-9b0c-83f0e901feca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394833 #edb/ref 1080 "images/43/438928da5e9de5e9ba3d2abbe06b6f1f8b6b0598a0b82bec1f51824b9ef72a71.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394833 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394833 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "547435a9-62b0-54fe-88c5-c2df65965648" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-105291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396585 #edb/ref 1071 "The set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "faa7ddb4-5b88-5e56-a53e-35fdb9d2ef93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396585 #edb/ref 1074 "We say that two vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are *linearly independent* if the only solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{v}_{1}+{x}_{2}{v}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nis ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Otherwise, the vectors are *linearly dependent*.\n\nA set containing *only two* vectors is linearly *dependent* if and only if the two vectors are parallel.\n\nFrom the given options, the correct answer is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo see why, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}.\n$$\n\nThis way, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1}-{a}_{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are parallel.\n\nTo see why this means that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly dependent, we notice that the equation\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}=0\n$$\n\nhas the solution ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1,$ which is different from ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the set is linearly dependent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186396591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186396590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396586 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a01010bc-5e11-583a-98fc-7b20994c0fc5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396587 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396587 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396587 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e5f3b240-c702-580d-9cdf-43123253f773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396588 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396588 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396588 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "574dea89-ee9d-5556-a72b-c257c732699e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396589 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396589 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396589 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "640a91d9-57f7-5e99-bc15-29e1456e8af0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396590 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396590 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396590 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2044901c-e971-534a-af35-fba5ec803324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396591 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396591 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396591 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3bf8a5fb-65f3-58d7-a288-3f0a87f9681a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396708 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186396709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396708 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78297" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396708 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396708 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following represents a reflection across the $x$-axis?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396708 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1040f4bd-3d1a-5f7b-8b2b-4c581d4ad345" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396708 #edb/ref 1074 "The correct answer is $T=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Indeed, consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors, as shown below. \n\n![](images/9e/9e322e6ed1ddb8f1d094633e931cd179b7eb9d6f47acd7c7d0809f113df91b22.png)\n\nNow, we can find the images of ${i,j}$ under $T$, as