396712 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e8c0ccfc-1a58-56d8-bf17-b789f1ca213c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396713 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396713 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396713 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c8045e89-1656-55f6-a0dd-d7b1b98bab9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396714 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396714 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186396714 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d3f965ef-6b47-527f-a571-556003d4b579" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398834 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186398835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398834 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47077" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398834 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398834 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that the function $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}x+1, & x\\le 1 \\\\ 5-2x, & x>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the intermediate value theorem be applied to the function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398834 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f66078a-9fc7-5c7e-ae29-7151d1232eff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398834 #edb/ref 1074 "The intermediate value theorem can be applied to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nThe function is discontinuous at $x=1.$ To see this, we note that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)=2\\ne 3={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x).\n$$\n\n \n\nWe conclude that the intermediate value theorem can be applied to the function $f(x)$ only on closed intervals that do not contain the point $x=1.$ \n\nFrom the given options, only the interval $[-2,0]$ is allowed.  The intervals $(1,3]$ and $[-2,1)$ are not closed, $[-2,1]$ and $[0,3]$ contain the point $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186398836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186398835 #edb/ref 